 "wiki": {
  "title": "Cape Cross thick-toed gecko",
  "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Cross_thick-toed_gecko",
  "revision": 1369477531,
  "sections": [
   {
    "h": null,
    "level": 1,
    "paras": [
     "The Cape Cross thick-toed gecko (Pachydactylus kochii), also known commonly as Koch's gecko and Koch's thick-toed gecko, is a species of lizard in the family Gekkonidae. The species is endemic to southern Africa."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Etymology",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "The specific name, kochii, is in honor of Austrian-born South African entomologist Charles Koch."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Geographic range",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "P. kochii is found in Namibia and in South Africa (southern Namaqualand)."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Habitat",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "The preferred natural habitats of P. kochii are desert and savanna, at altitudes of 10–800 m (33–2,625 ft)."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Description",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "Adults of P. kochii have a snout-to-vent length (SVL) of 4–5 cm (1.6–2.0 in). The body is slender. Dorsally, the ground color is grayish with a lavender tinge, and there are five reddish-brown crossbands. Ventrally it is white."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Behavior",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "P. kochii is terrestrial. It shelters in burrows dug by other animals."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Reproduction",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "P. kochii is oviparous."
    ]
   }
  ]
 }
}